ric practice within  Islam a complicate in this binary allows  us an understanding of the different  realms of women's religious expression  and the ways they engage religious  authority and activism in the article I  also draw on hagiographical literature  to explore the traditional patterns of  female spirituality I am interested in  the conjunctures of tradition and  modernity and the continuity of Sufism  as a postmodern reality and how that  shifts the dynamics of female spiritual  expression okay let's start with the  word Sufism tell us what that is exactly  so Sufism is generally defined as  Islamic mysticism or the spiritual or  esoteric dimension of Islam that  incorporates techniques by which a  person seeks a direct or a personal  encounter with the divine what kind of  techniques it's like its devotional  practices it's kind of like meditation  okay so does it vary dramatically from  Islam  so-so so basically it is it is it is a  tradition within this law okay so by the  11th or 12th centuries Sufism that  started as a spiritual movement  developed into organizational structures  currently known as Sufi orders or  their hoods and these structures have  over periods of history served as major  scholarly and spiritual centers of  edification within Islam and have had  major social and political roles  so basically Sufism is oftentimes cast  as a as the quietest or disengaged  practice of a group of the marginal and  group of God's seekers  however place in Sufism within the  broader and varied contexts of the  Islamic tradition reveals that Sufis  have actually produced complex systems  of thought and elaborate interpretive  traditions and that the Sufi tradition  embraces a whole range of political  social institutional hermeneutical  literary aesthetic features within the  Islamic tradition and this understanding  of the Sufi tradition also helps us to  situate women's roles within the broader  or the complex discursive formations of  religious authority in Islam okay so how  did you become involved in doing this  kind of work what led you to it  so first because of my academic interest  and the forums of and changes of female  Islamic authority but I've been  interested in this particular topic  because Moroccan women's historical  participation in Sufism has received  very little scholarly attention okay the  primary sources that we have for these  roles and practices are basically  historical or hagiographic accounts  where of course male subjectivity is  dominate and especially that these works  are written by men so there's little  space that is devoted for women's  spirituality  there's also anthropological cities and  Travel literature or accounts and these  usually have Orientalist motives and  have contributed largely to constructing  the binary of official or formal Islam  that is male rational legal as opposed  to popular or informal Islam that is  female irrational and and spiritual so  there there is there is some lost  narrative there that needs to be  retrieved  thankfully a recent scholarship has  started food has started to account for  the nuances and the differences within  women's religious and spiritual  expression another motive is of course  personal as a person of faith who seeks  to fit in some form of spiritual space  and navigates the complexity of the  experience I do believe in the  importance of claiming and narrative  that tends to be unheard in the loud  rhetoric surrounding the woman question  and the dominant discourses that tend to  that tend to define women social roles  and Muslim contacts so talk about the  field work you did to do this paper so I  conducted ethnographic fieldwork for the  period of three years or even more  because of my personal interest in the  tradition so the purpose of this  fieldwork was to investigate women's  participation within Sufi orders and how  this participation both complicates and  sustains perceptions of the gender space  and the gendered practice of spiritual  authority so it was very interesting to  look at the  the discrepancies between the commonly  held belief that women are excluded from  positions of leadership and public  performance and how these women get to  define leadership and public performance  so I conducted fieldwork within  different Sufi orders and across  different regions and the country so it  was very fascinating to see that women  participate in significant numbers and  the tradition and that the tradition  itself is diverse and that there exists  multiple models of participation and  leadership and and and that there are  several avenues for women to practice  Authority and get recognized as  spiritual authorities so women  participate in communal and devotional  practices there are a lot of women who  serve as representatives of main male  authorities within their Sufi orders so  they oversee communal activities and  they determined the affiliation of  future adherence there's so many women  who serve as spiritual teachers although  women's spiritual leadership is excluded  in formal hierarchies within Sufi orders  but they're there a lot of women who are  recognized as spiritual authorities by  their followers and disciples both men  and women and I was also interested to  study these patterns in parallel with  fieldwork I conducted with sponsored  Imams and women activists within  Islamist movements and just observed  that these religious trends are not  homogeneous but they're not entirely  divergent either and this really helps  us understand the Islamic tradition and  women's roles within the tradition  beyond  essentialist and monolithic perception  very interesting so in Sufism is their  gender equality between men and women  well because it is a spiritual realm  where there is a strong argument that  there's absolute equality between men  and women because it's Sorrell where  only souls are recognized but not social  or biological differences but of course  the tradition is shaped by  socio-cultural circumstances so what are  you finding it's it's not homogeneous as  I said I conducted fieldwork in  different regions of the country where  you of course get different cultures as  well so there are settings that are more  welcoming and open to women and actually  and basically offer equal space for both  men and women like I attended communal  gatherings where I would be the only  woman and I would be seated in the same  space as the man and I would be served  tea and food by the men and so I've been  into those very welcoming and equal  spaces but I'll let you go to other  places where of course the culture is  more conservative and and I think it's  also it's also due to the fact that big  Sufi orders have have sort of structure  as to where men get to choose to have  their own space and conduct and oversee  their own activities so so okay all  right so what then are some of the  interactions between Sufism and politics  in Morocco  well the contested relationship between  Sufism and the states in Morocco's as  oldest and some Moroccan history over  periods of history  some Sufi orders have challenged  political authorities manifested  political ambition and established  connections with colonial powers while  other Sufi orders demonstrated absolute  support to the state in Morocco's recent  history especially in the context of of  the country's engagement and  counter-radicalization strategies after  the country was hit by a major terrorist  attack back in 2003 Sufi orders  re-emerged on the scene as a new and  powerful social and political force and  established a massive recruitment across  different social categories and among  both men and women so Sufism is  currently promoted as a tolerant  peaceful and apolitical  form of religiosity and which is the  opposite of excuse me yes yeah and and  it is exactly and it is enjoying great  super great state support because for  the very reason that it's considered as  a non oppositional religious trend  Sufism is also heavily promoted in the  media local and international  conferences and Sufi music festivals  organized all over the country to spread  and intellectualize debate about Sufism  and carriage aesthetic aesthetic  performance in 2011 Sufi or Sufi orders  emerged back on the scene as a  significant player in the context of the  Arab Spring uprisings so after King  Mohammed the six responded to popular  protests by drafting a modified  constitution Sufi orders urged their  followers to march in large numbers  industries in support of the new  constitution and the monarchy and this  shows the close interactions between  religion and politics in Morocco and  also serves to reiterate Morocco's sufi  oriented religious identity and  counterbalance oppositional voices  included in the growing as long as two  movements now you mentioned that women  can enjoy some form of leadership  through Sufism does that cross over into  these you know the secular world in  terms of politics do you see more women  because of their role in Sufism becoming  more have more leadership roles in  politics for instance well the political  sphere in Morocco is or the public  sphere in Morocco is a very  heterogeneous sphere so you get  different players and actors you have  political actors that include both as  long as in secular actors and you have  formal religious structures that now  include Sufism so you get different  players who have been performing in the  public sphere since the earlier history  of Morocco  especially after independence so I  personally what I find interesting is  that the institutionalization of certain  forms of Islam has contributed to  providing more visibility to certain  female actors but of course women have  been active in the public sphere since  earlier right right but I what I think I  was trying to get at because Sufism is  supported by the state do you see more  women more sufi women becoming more  active in the political parts of morocco  you see what I'm saying  you mean within the mainstream political  yeah yes yeah not in that sense because  Sufism presents itself as an apolitical  form of religious expression so it's not  a movement that would preach involvement  in political structures although  interestingly enough it can show up on  the political sphere to support the  states when necessary right but you  don't you don't really get Sufis aren't  carriage - part of the political parties  or or participate visibly actively  within the formal political sphere  although these spaces do overlap and  Sufism it's not this this this really  disengaged in a political religious  expression but it does interact with  politics in different ways okay so what  do you conclude in your paper well I I  don't know if I have a conclusion in my  paper this paper was just just sort of a  beginning in this research and it has  inspired me to do more research on this  yet uncharted terrain of research  basically so I hope to spend some of my  time here at Yale to work on pre-modern  sources and hagiographies of Sufi women  to sort of create a it sort of  constructs a historical narrative of how  gender operated and pre-modern in modern  Sufis or circles in Morocco I am  interested in recovering patterns of  women's spiritual expression and modes  of religious authority and  how that informs contemporary Sufi  practices well very interesting thank  you so much for being here with us today  I'm sharing some of your work thanks for  having me for more information about  Professor L Hitomi and her research  please visit our website at macmillan  report yale.edu be sure to join us again  for another episode of the Macmillan  report made possible through funding  from the Whitney and Betty Macmillan  Center for International and area  studies at Yale  you

